WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.466
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:08.979
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:17.132
Unit forty-seven: Wealth, Work and Leisure. In this unit, you will speculate about the future of work and leisure.

4
00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:19.652
leisure society.

5
00:00:20.352 --> 00:00:27.077
leisure society means a predicted society where machines do most work and people have more free time.

6
00:00:27.377 --> 00:00:33.742
Economists in the 1930s predicted a leisure society by 2030.

7
00:00:34.442 --> 00:00:38.092
Your turn. Say: leisure society.

8
00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:42.235
leisure society.

9
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.367
work ethic.

10
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:49.226
work ethic means a belief in the moral value of hard work.

11
00:00:49.526 --> 00:00:52.524
Her strong work ethic impressed every employer.

12
00:00:53.224 --> 00:00:56.086
Your turn. Say: work ethic.

13
00:00:58.786 --> 00:00:59.903
work ethic.

14
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:02.045
idleness.

15
00:01:02.745 --> 00:01:05.871
idleness means doing nothing; not working.

16
00:01:06.171 --> 00:01:10.470
Some philosophers defended idleness as the root of creativity.

17
00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.135
Your turn. Say: idleness.

18
00:01:16.835 --> 00:01:17.848
idleness.

19
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.234
overwork culture.

20
00:01:20.934 --> 00:01:25.186
overwork culture means a workplace culture that expects very long hours.

21
00:01:25.486 --> 00:01:28.298
Overwork culture leads to burnout and poor health.

22
00:01:28.998 --> 00:01:32.173
Your turn. Say: overwork culture.

23
00:01:34.873 --> 00:01:36.223
overwork culture.

24
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:38.601
time affluence.

25
00:01:39.301 --> 00:01:43.135
time affluence means the feeling of having enough free time.

26
00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:47.849
Research links time affluence to greater happiness than extra money.

27
00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:51.573
Your turn. Say: time affluence.

28
00:01:54.273 --> 00:01:55.646
time affluence.

29
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:58.150
conspicuous leisure.

30
00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:04.588
conspicuous leisure means showing off free time as a sign of wealth or status.

31
00:02:04.888 --> 00:02:08.618
Long holidays posted online can be conspicuous leisure.

32
00:02:09.318 --> 00:02:12.655
Your turn. Say: conspicuous leisure.

33
00:02:15.355 --> 00:02:16.797
conspicuous leisure.

34
00:02:17.783 --> 00:02:19.249
meaningful leisure.

35
00:02:19.949 --> 00:02:25.095
meaningful leisure means free time used for activities that bring purpose and growth.

36
00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:28.776
Volunteering and learning music are meaningful leisure.

37
00:02:29.476 --> 00:02:32.860
Your turn. Say: meaningful leisure.

38
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.944
meaningful leisure.

39
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.392
right to disconnect.

40
00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:45.424
right to disconnect means a legal right not to answer work messages outside working hours.

41
00:02:45.724 --> 00:02:50.278
France introduced a right to disconnect in 2017.

42
00:02:50.978 --> 00:02:54.141
Your turn. Say: right to disconnect.

43
00:02:56.841 --> 00:02:58.237
right to disconnect.

44
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.644
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:01.944 --> 00:03:06.243
Rithy, Keynes predicted we would work fifteen hours a week by now.

46
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.722
Had he seen today's overwork culture, he'd have been shocked.

47
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:16.097
Indeed. Why do you think the leisure society never arrived?

48
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:21.391
Were productivity gains shared fairly, we might already have it.

49
00:03:22.683 --> 00:03:25.765
Interesting. And if you had more time affluence?

50
00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:31.546
I'd learn the tro, assuming my boss respected my right to disconnect!

51
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.918
Should automation spread, attitudes may change.

52
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:41.759
Otherwise, we'll keep working long hours long after machines could free us.

53
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.408
Grammar focus: Conditionals beyond the rules (extended).

54
00:03:46.708 --> 00:03:49.834
Advanced conditionals add variety and formality.

55
00:03:50.134 --> 00:03:54.200
Had Keynes seen today's overwork culture, he'd have been shocked.

56
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.820
Were productivity gains shared fairly, we might already have a leisure society.

57
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:07.355
Inversion (Had, Were, Should) and words like 'assuming', 'provided' and 'otherwise' make conditionals more formal and varied.

58
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:12.187
Now read and listen. Whatever Happened to the Leisure Society?.

59
00:04:12.487 --> 00:04:19.989
In 1930, John Maynard Keynes predicted that his grandchildren would work only about fifteen hours a week.

60
00:04:21.283 --> 00:04:26.360
Productivity has indeed multiplied, yet the leisure society has not arrived.

61
00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:33.876
Instead, many professionals live within an overwork culture, where long hours signal commitment.

62
00:04:35.183 --> 00:04:41.467
Part of the explanation lies in a powerful work ethic that treats idleness as a moral failure.

63
00:04:42.783 --> 00:04:48.359
Another lies in consumption: had we accepted simpler lifestyles, we could have worked less.

64
00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:56.479
Psychologists report that time affluence contributes more to wellbeing than modest increases in income.

65
00:04:57.783 --> 00:05:03.440
Some countries now protect a right to disconnect, while others experiment with four-day weeks.

66
00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:12.833
Should these experiments succeed, meaningful leisure might finally replace conspicuous leisure as a symbol of the good life.

67
00:05:14.117 --> 00:05:16.955
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:17.255 --> 00:05:19.939
The feeling of having enough free time is blank.

69
00:05:20.239 --> 00:05:25.181
A, time affluence. B, idleness. C, work ethic.

70
00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:32.100
The answer is A: time affluence.

71
00:05:33.617 --> 00:05:35.976
blank he known, he would have helped.

72
00:05:36.276 --> 00:05:39.546
A, Had. B, Were. C, Should.

73
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.259
The answer is A: Had.

74
00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.667
'Should you need help, call me' means blank.

75
00:05:49.967 --> 00:05:55.129
A, if you need help. B, you must need help. C, you needed help.

76
00:05:59.117 --> 00:06:01.232
The answer is A: if you need help.

77
00:06:02.717 --> 00:06:06.539
A legal right not to answer work messages after hours is the blank.

78
00:06:06.839 --> 00:06:12.838
A, right to disconnect. B, leisure society. C, overwork culture.

79
00:06:16.850 --> 00:06:19.651
The answer is A: right to disconnect.

80
00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:26.284
According to the text, how many hours a week did Keynes predict people would work?

81
00:06:26.584 --> 00:06:31.039
A, about fifteen. B, about thirty. C, about forty.

82
00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.967
The answer is A: about fifteen.

83
00:06:39.483 --> 00:06:44.934
Now it's your turn. Part one: Would you choose a four-day working week with lower pay?

84
00:06:50.234 --> 00:06:56.599
Part two: Use one inverted conditional and one alternative such as 'assuming' or 'otherwise'.

85
00:07:02.899 --> 00:07:14.551
Were I offered a four-day week, I would probably accept, assuming the pay cut were small. Time affluence matters. Otherwise, I would have little time for family and meaningful leisure.

86
00:07:14.851 --> 00:07:16.828
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:17.817 --> 00:07:23.116
Great work today! You learned eight new words and conditionals beyond the rules (extended).

88
00:07:23.416 --> 00:07:28.448
Today's value is healthy body. Move your body every day.

89
00:07:28.748 --> 00:07:31.703
See you in Unit forty-eight. Goodbye!

90
00:07:32.003 --> 00:07:35.108
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

